{"id": "y0887", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 12, "onset_chunk": 10, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so Matthew gave equal numbers of crackers and cake s to his 28 friends. He started with 13 crack ers and 15 cakes. Since he gave them equally, each friend got the same amount of both items. But here' s the thing— if you divide 13 crackers by 2 8 friends, you get less than one cracker per person, which doesn't make sense for eating. Similarly, 15 cake s divided by 28 friends is also less than one cake per person. So it seems like there might be a mistake in the question or maybe we're missing some details. As it stands, no one could have eaten a whole number of crackers or cakes since they didn't split evenly among all 28 friends. Maybe he only gave out part s? Or perhaps not everyone got something?", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 48, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "y0895", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 12, "onset_chunk": 11, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So Jerry started with eight action figures. Then he added four more, so that makes eight plus four, which is twelve. But then he removed five old ones. So from twelve, if you take away five, you get seven. There were seven action figures on his shelf in the end.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 15, "carrier_ended": null}
