{"id": "y1119", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 15, "onset_chunk": 15, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "The correct answer is A. Expectations of surpluses of goods in the future will cause the aggregate demand curve to shift to the left. When people expect there to be a surplus of goods, they tend to delay their purchases, which reduces current demand. B, a decrease in income taxes, would actually increase consumer spending and shift the curve to the right. C, an increase in government spending also shifts the curve to the right because it increases overall demand. D, an increase in foreign income, typically leads to higher demand for export s, which again shift s the curve to the right. So only option A represents a factor that would lead to a leftward shift in the aggregate demand curve.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 38, "carrier_ended": null}
